<section class="info-card" id="particle-behavior">
<h2>Penetration Is Depth; Deposition Is Where Particles Stop</h2>
<p><strong>Penetration</strong> describes how deeply an aerosol travels into the respiratory tract. <strong>Deposition</strong> describes where particles leave the carrier gas and remain on airway or alveolar surfaces.</p>
<div class="component-grid" style="margin-top:16px">
<div class="example-box soft-coral"><strong>Inertial impaction</strong><br/>Larger, faster particles cannot follow sharp airway turns and strike upper or central airway surfaces.</div>
<div class="example-box soft-teal"><strong>Gravitational sedimentation</strong><br/>Particles settle onto airway surfaces when airflow slows, especially during a breath hold.</div>
<div class="example-box soft-blue"><strong>Diffusion</strong><br/>Very small particles move randomly and may deposit in the peripheral lung or be exhaled.</div>
</div>
<div class="callout"><strong>Respirable range:</strong> Particles approximately 1–5 μm are most useful for delivery to lower and smaller airways. Larger particles tend to impact in the mouth and upper airway; extremely small particles may remain suspended and be exhaled.</div>

<article class="activity-card require-complete" data-activity-id="pharm_1_4_particle_match" data-activity-name="Match Aerosol Concepts" data-activity-type="sorting" data-required="true" style="margin-top:16px">
