<section class="info-card board" id="breathing-pattern">
<h2>Breathing Pattern Determines Where the Drug Goes</h2>
<p>For an MDI or nebulized aerosol, a <strong>slow, deep inspiration</strong> reduces turbulent flow and upper-airway impaction. A breath hold—when the patient can safely perform one—allows more time for sedimentation.</p>
<div class="two-grid" style="margin-top:16px">
<div class="example-box soft-teal"><strong>MDI or nebulized aerosol</strong><br/>Slow, deep inhalation followed by a breath hold of up to about 10 seconds as tolerated.</div>
<div class="example-box soft-blue"><strong>DPI</strong><br/>A quick, deep, forceful inhalation is usually required to disperse the dry powder.</div>
</div>
<div class="callout"><strong>Do not apply one breathing pattern to every device.</strong> A slow inhalation benefits an MDI; a DPI depends on adequate inspiratory flow and a faster, forceful breath.</div>

<article class="activity-card require-complete" data-activity-id="pharm_1_4_breathing_pattern" data-activity-name="Choose the Optimal Breathing Pattern" data-activity-type="clinical-application" data-required="true" style="margin-top:16px">
<h3>Choose the Optimal Breathing Pattern</h3>
<p>A patient is using an MDI with a valved holding chamber. Which instruction best supports lower-airway deposition?</p>
<div class="quiz-options">
<button class="quiz-option single-check-option" data-correct="false" data-feedback="A rapid inhalation increases turbulence and impaction in the mouth and central airways.">Inhale as quickly as possible and exhale immediately.</button>
<button class="quiz-option single-check-option" data-correct="true" data-feedback="Correct. Slow, deep inhalation and a brief breath hold improve penetration and sedimentation.">Inhale slowly and deeply, then hold the breath as tolerated.</button>
<button class="quiz-option single-check-option" data-correct="false" data-feedback="A shallow breath limits penetration to smaller airways.">Use a slow, shallow breath without a breath hold.</button>
</div>
<div class="quiz-feedback"></div><div class="activity-status" id="breathingStatus">Select the best instruction.</div>
</article>
</section>

<section class="info-card rt" id="mdi">
<h2>Metered-Dose Inhalers, Spacers, and Valved Holding Chambers</h2>
<p>An MDI releases a metered plume under pressure. The patient must coordinate actuation with the start of a slow inspiration unless a breath-actuated device is used.</p>
<div class="three-card-grid" style="margin-top:16px">
<div class="example-box soft-teal"><strong>Spacer</strong><br/>Adds distance between the MDI and mouth, allowing the plume to slow and larger particles to settle.</div>
<div class="example-box soft-blue"><strong>Valved holding chamber</strong><br/>Includes a one-way valve and temporarily holds aerosol, reducing the need for precise hand–breath coordination.</div>
<div class="example-box soft-amber"><strong>Electrostatic charge</strong><br/>Some plastic chambers attract aerosol to their walls. Follow cleaning and preparation instructions for the specific device.</div>
</div>
<p>A chamber does <strong>not</strong> increase aerosol velocity. It reduces velocity, limits oropharyngeal impaction, and improves the chance that respirable particles enter the lungs.</p>

<article class="activity-card require-complete" data-activity-id="pharm_1_4_spacer_check" data-activity-name="Explain Spacer Benefits" data-activity-type="knowledge-check" data-required="true" style="margin-top:16px">
<h3>Explain Spacer Benefits</h3>
<p>Which statement is <strong>not</strong> an advantage of a spacer or holding chamber?</p>
<div class="quiz-options">
<button class="quiz-option single-check-option" data-correct="true" data-feedback="Correct. A spacer slows the aerosol plume rather than increasing particle velocity.">It increases aerosol-particle flow speed through the chamber.</button>
<button class="quiz-option single-check-option" data-correct="false" data-feedback="A chamber reduces the need for precise coordination.">It improves coordination between actuation and inhalation.</button>
<button class="quiz-option single-check-option" data-correct="false" data-feedback="Larger particles can settle in the device, reducing mouth and throat deposition.">It reduces oropharyngeal impaction.</button>
</div>
<div class="quiz-feedback"></div><div class="activity-status" id="spacerStatus">Identify the incorrect claim.</div>
</article>

<h3 style="margin-top:20px">Core MDI technique</h3>
<div class="data-card">
<ol>
<li>Verify the medication, dose counter, expiration, and device instructions.</li>
<li>Remove the cap, inspect the mouthpiece, and shake when directed.</li>
<li>Prime if the inhaler is new or has not been used for the manufacturer-specified interval.</li>
<li>Exhale fully away from the device.</li>
<li>Seal lips around the mouthpiece or chamber.</li>
<li>Begin a slow inhalation and actuate once as inspiration begins.</li>
<li>Continue inhaling slowly and deeply.</li>
<li>Hold the breath as tolerated, then exhale slowly.</li>
<li>Wait the product-recommended interval before another actuation.</li>
</ol>
</div>

<article class="activity-card require-complete" data-activity-id="pharm_1_4_mdi_sequence" data-activity-name="Sequence MDI Technique" data-activity-type="sorting" data-required="true" style="margin-top:16px">
<h3>Sequence the Critical MDI Steps</h3>
<div class="sort-grid">
<div class="sort-row" data-answer="1"><span>Exhale fully away from the inhaler</span><select><option value="">Order</option><option value="1">1</option><option value="2">2</option><option value="3">3</option><option value="4">4</option></select></div>
<div class="sort-row" data-answer="2"><span>Begin a slow inspiration and actuate once</span><select><option value="">Order</option><option value="1">1</option><option value="2">2</option><option value="3">3</option><option value="4">4</option></select></div>
<div class="sort-row" data-answer="3"><span>Continue a slow, deep inhalation</span><select><option value="">Order</option><option value="1">1</option><option value="2">2</option><option value="3">3</option><option value="4">4</option></select></div>
<div class="sort-row" data-answer="4"><span>Hold the breath as tolerated</span><select><option value="">Order</option><option value="1">1</option><option value="2">2</option><option value="3">3</option><option value="4">4</option></select></div>
</div>
<button class="btn btn-primary" id="checkMdiSequence" type="button" style="margin-top:14px">Check Sequence</button>
<div class="activity-status" id="mdiSequenceStatus">Arrange the four steps.</div>
</article>

<div class="callout"><strong>Without a chamber:</strong> The preferred technique is generally a closed-mouth seal with coordinated slow inhalation. Some older instruction sets describe holding the MDI a few centimeters from an open mouth; use that only when consistent with current product instructions and local policy.</div>
</section>

<section class="info-card" id="device-selection">
<h2>Select the Device the Patient Can Actually Use</h2>
<p>The “best” aerosol device is the one that can deliver the prescribed drug effectively for that patient in that setting.</p>
<div class="data-card">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Patient factor</th><th>What to assess</th><th>Likely implication</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Young child</td><td>Coordination, mask seal, caregiver technique</td><td>MDI with valved holding chamber and age-appropriate interface is often effective</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weak inspiratory effort</td><td>Peak inspiratory flow and neuromuscular strength</td><td>A DPI may not disperse adequately</td></tr>
<tr><td>Altered mental status</td><td>Ability to follow directions and seal around a mouthpiece</td><td>Use a device that can deliver during tidal breathing, such as a nebulizer with mask</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mechanical ventilation</td><td>Artificial airway, circuit, humidification, ventilator pattern</td><td>Use an in-line adapter or nebulizer positioned and timed appropriately</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<article class="activity-card require-complete" data-activity-id="pharm_1_4_device_selection" data-activity-name="Select the Delivery Device" data-activity-type="clinical-application" data-required="true" style="margin-top:16px">
<h3>Select the Delivery Device</h3>
<p>A 3-year-old requires inhaled bronchodilator therapy at home. The child can breathe calmly through a mask but cannot coordinate MDI actuation.</p>
<div class="quiz-options">
<button class="quiz-option single-check-option" data-correct="true" data-feedback="Correct. An MDI with a valved holding chamber and age-appropriate mask reduces coordination demands and is effective when used correctly.">MDI with valved holding chamber and mask</button>
<button class="quiz-option single-check-option" data-correct="false" data-feedback="A DPI requires sufficient inspiratory flow and cooperation, which may be unreliable at this age.">DPI</button>
<button class="quiz-option single-check-option" data-correct="false" data-feedback="A bare MDI requires more coordination and is less appropriate for this child.">MDI without a chamber</button>
</div>
<div class="quiz-feedback"></div><div class="activity-status" id="deviceStatus">Choose the most appropriate home device.</div>
</article>
</section>

<section class="info-card board" id="dpi-nebulizers">
<h2>Dry-Powder Inhalers and Nebulizers</h2>
<h3>Dry-powder inhalers</h3>
<p>A DPI is breath-actuated. The patient must generate enough inspiratory flow to disperse the powder, understand the steps, and avoid exhaling into the device.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Assess before choosing a DPI:</strong> inspiratory flow, mental status, neuromuscular strength, hand function, and ability to follow instructions. Device-specific flow requirements vary; do not assume one threshold applies to every DPI.</div>
<p><strong>Technique:</strong> prepare the dose, exhale away from the device, seal the lips, inhale quickly and deeply, hold the breath as tolerated, and close the device. Tiotropium is available in capsule-based HandiHaler delivery, but the capsule is inhaled through the device and is never swallowed.</p>

<article class="activity-card require-complete" data-activity-id="pharm_1_4_dpi_check" data-activity-name="Assess DPI Readiness" data-activity-type="clinical-application" data-required="true" style="margin-top:16px">
<h3>Assess DPI Readiness</h3>
<p>Which finding most directly suggests that a patient may not receive an adequate dose from a DPI?</p>
<div class="quiz-options">
<button class="quiz-option single-check-option" data-correct="true" data-feedback="Correct. Insufficient inspiratory flow may fail to disperse and carry the dry powder into the lungs.">Severe neuromuscular weakness with low inspiratory flow</button>
<button class="quiz-option single-check-option" data-correct="false" data-feedback="A patient who follows directions and generates adequate inspiratory flow may be a good DPI candidate.">Good cognition and strong inspiratory effort</button>
<button class="quiz-option single-check-option" data-correct="false" data-feedback="A breath hold may support deposition after the powder is inhaled.">Ability to hold the breath briefly</button>
</div>
<div class="quiz-feedback"></div><div class="activity-status" id="dpiStatus">Identify the limiting factor.</div>
</article>

<h3 style="margin-top:22px">Nebulizers</h3>
<div class="three-card-grid" style="margin-top:14px">
<div class="example-box soft-blue"><strong>SVN</strong><br/>A small-volume jet nebulizer continuously generates aerosol. Typical external flow is often around 6–8 L/min, but follow the device specification.</div>
<div class="example-box soft-teal"><strong>BAN</strong><br/>A breath-actuated nebulizer generates aerosol mainly during inspiration, reducing medication wasted during exhalation.</div>
<div class="example-box soft-amber"><strong>Interface matters</strong><br/>A mouthpiece generally improves lung delivery when the patient can use it; a well-fitted mask is appropriate when cooperation is limited.</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="info-card red" id="medication-sequence">
<h2>Medication Sequence and Safety Monitoring</h2>
<p>When multiple inhaled medications are prescribed, sequence them according to the clinical goal and current orders. A rapid bronchodilator is commonly given before an inhaled anti-inflammatory or secretion-clearance medication when opening the airway will improve delivery or reduce bronchospasm risk.</p>
<div class="two-grid" style="margin-top:16px">
<div class="example-box soft-teal"><strong>Bronchodilator first</strong><br/>Albuterol may improve airway caliber before corticosteroids, mucolytics, or other inhaled therapies.</div>
<div class="example-box soft-blue"><strong>Mucolytics</strong><br/>Reduce secretion viscosity or disrupt mucus structure; they do not directly reverse bronchospasm.</div>
</div>
<h3 style="margin-top:20px">Continuous albuterol</h3>
<p>High-dose or continuous beta-2 agonist therapy requires close monitoring. Expected risks include tachycardia, tremor, arrhythmias, and hypokalemia. Beta-2 stimulation tends to shift potassium into cells and may raise rather than lower glucose.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Reassess:</strong> heart rate and rhythm, breath sounds, work of breathing, airflow measures when available, potassium, glucose, and the ongoing need for continuous therapy.</div>
</section>

<section class="info-card rt" id="ventilator-delivery">
<h2>Aerosol Delivery During Mechanical Ventilation</h2>
<p>The ventilator circuit creates additional opportunities for medication loss. Device position, humidification, bias flow, inspiratory timing, artificial airway diameter, and circuit geometry all affect delivery.</p>
<div class="data-card">
<ol>
<li>Verify the medication, dose, indication, and compatible delivery device.</li>
<li>Remove an HME from the aerosol path during treatment when required; an HME can trap medication.</li>
<li>Place the MDI adapter or nebulizer in the inspiratory limb at the recommended distance from the patient, commonly upstream from the wye rather than on the expiratory limb.</li>
<li>Coordinate MDI actuation with the beginning of inspiration.</li>
<li>For an SVN, use the manufacturer-recommended driving flow and account for added circuit flow.</li>
<li>Consider whether bias flow or flow triggering reduces aerosol available to the patient; adjust only when safe and consistent with the ventilator/device protocol.</li>
<li>Monitor pressures, delivered volume, alarms, synchrony, and oxygenation.</li>
<li>Restore the circuit and humidification configuration after treatment.</li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="callout"><strong>Breath holds and extended inspiratory time:</strong> They may improve deposition, but they can shorten expiratory time. In obstructive disease, assess for incomplete exhalation and auto-PEEP.</div>
</section>

<section class="case-card require-complete" id="case" data-activity-id="pharm_1_4_ventilator_case" data-activity-name="Aerosol Delivery During Mechanical Ventilation" data-activity-type="guided-case" data-required="true">
<span class="eyebrow">Guided Clinical Reasoning</span>
<h2>Aerosol Delivery During Mechanical Ventilation</h2>
<p>An intubated patient with severe bronchospasm has an HME at the wye. The order is for an MDI bronchodilator. The patient has obstructive disease and already demonstrates prolonged exhalation.</p>
<div class="quiz-options">
<button class="quiz-option case-option" data-correct="false" data-feedback="The HME can trap aerosol, and the expiratory limb is not the appropriate medication-delivery location.">Leave the HME in place and actuate the MDI into the expiratory limb.</button>
<button class="quiz-option case-option" data-correct="true" data-feedback="Correct. Remove the HME from the medication path, place an appropriate adapter in the inspiratory limb, synchronize actuation with inspiration, and monitor for incomplete exhalation or auto-PEEP.">Remove the HME from the aerosol path, use an inspiratory-limb adapter, coordinate actuation with inspiration, and reassess expiratory flow.</button>
<button class="quiz-option case-option" data-correct="false" data-feedback="A prolonged inspiratory hold may worsen air trapping in this obstructive patient. Technique must balance deposition with adequate exhalation.">Add a long inspiratory hold after every actuation without monitoring exhalation.</button>
</div>
<div class="quiz-feedback"></div><div class="activity-status" id="caseStatus">Choose the safest delivery plan.</div>
</section>

<section class="quiz-card require-complete" id="knowledge-check" data-activity-id="pharm_1_4_knowledge_check" data-activity-name="Aerosol Delivery Mastery" data-activity-type="knowledge-check" data-required="true">
<h2>Aerosol Delivery Mastery</h2>

<div class="knowledge-question"><h3>1. The depth an aerosol particle reaches is called:</h3><div class="quiz-options">
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="1" data-correct="true" data-feedback="Correct. Penetration describes depth of travel.">Penetration</button>
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="1" data-correct="false" data-feedback="Deposition describes where the particle stops and remains.">Deposition</button>
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="1" data-correct="false" data-feedback="Impaction is one mechanism of deposition.">Inertial impaction</button>
</div><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div>

<div class="knowledge-question"><h3>2. The ideal particle range for smaller-airway delivery is approximately:</h3><div class="quiz-options">
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="2" data-correct="true" data-feedback="Correct. Particles in the 1–5 μm range are generally respirable.">1–5 μm</button>
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="2" data-correct="false" data-feedback="Particles this large usually impact in the upper airway.">10–15 μm</button>
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="2" data-correct="false" data-feedback="Very small particles may remain suspended and be exhaled.">0.1–0.5 μm only</button>
</div><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div>

<div class="knowledge-question"><h3>3. A DPI generally requires which breathing pattern?</h3><div class="quiz-options">
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="3" data-correct="true" data-feedback="Correct. A forceful, deep inhalation disperses dry powder.">Fast and deep</button>
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="3" data-correct="false" data-feedback="Slow inhalation is preferred for an MDI, not most DPIs.">Slow and deep</button>
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="3" data-correct="false" data-feedback="A shallow breath limits delivery.">Fast and shallow</button>
</div><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div>

<div class="knowledge-question"><h3>4. Which device minimizes medication wasted during exhalation?</h3><div class="quiz-options">
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="4" data-correct="true" data-feedback="Correct. A breath-actuated nebulizer generates aerosol mainly during inspiration.">Breath-actuated nebulizer</button>
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="4" data-correct="false" data-feedback="A continuous-output SVN releases aerosol during both inspiration and expiration.">Continuous-output SVN</button>
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="4" data-correct="false" data-feedback="An MDI is a different device category and does not answer the nebulizer question.">MDI</button>
</div><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div>

<div class="knowledge-question"><h3>5. Which adverse effect is associated with continuous albuterol?</h3><div class="quiz-options">
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="5" data-correct="true" data-feedback="Correct. Beta-2 stimulation shifts potassium into cells and can cause hypokalemia.">Hypokalemia</button>
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="5" data-correct="false" data-feedback="Albuterol is more likely to increase glucose than cause hypoglycemia.">Hypoglycemia</button>
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="5" data-correct="false" data-feedback="Bradycardia is not an expected high-dose beta-agonist effect.">Bradycardia</button>
</div><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div>

<div class="knowledge-question"><h3>6. During ventilator aerosol delivery, an HME in the medication path should generally be:</h3><div class="quiz-options">
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="6" data-correct="true" data-feedback="Correct. The HME can filter or trap aerosol and should be removed from the aerosol path when the protocol requires it.">Removed during treatment, then restored afterward</button>
<button class="quiz-option knowledge-option" data-question="6" data-correct="false" data-feedback="Leaving it in place can substantially reduce delivered medication.">Left in place to increase deposition</button>
